wasm64 takes the x86_64 bindings, which is the Linux ABI the visorjs
kernel speaks on it.

Every per-arch module is selected by an explicit target_arch list with no
wasm64 arm, so on wasm64-visorjs-linux-musl `general`, `errno`, `ioctl` and
the rest are configured out and the crate fails with `unresolved import
crate::general`. rustix reaches it for every target_os = "linux".

The kernel and @reactor/libc on wasm64 use x86_64 syscall numbering, the
x86_64 struct stat and the x86_64 O_* / errno values (the libc crate's
wasm64 arm is written from the same headers), so the x86_64 modules are
the bindings, not an approximation of them. `char` is signed on wasm, so
no_std's c_char joins the c_schar list. No other target moves.

--- a/src/lib.rs
+++ b/src/lib.rs
@@ -37,6 +37,7 @@
         target_arch = "x86",
         target_arch = "x86_64",
         target_arch = "xtensa",
+        target_arch = "wasm64",
     ))]
     pub type c_char = c_schar;
 
@@ -1875,95 +1876,95 @@
 #[path = "x86/xdp.rs"]
 pub mod xdp;
 #[cfg(feature = "auxvec")]
-#[cfg(all(target_arch = "x86_64", target_pointer_width = "64"))]
+#[cfg(any(all(target_arch = "x86_64", target_pointer_width = "64"), target_arch = "wasm64"))]
 #[path = "x86_64/auxvec.rs"]
